# Shell scripts run under WSL dash, which fatals on CRLF ("set: Illegal
# option -"). Pin them to LF so no Windows-side editor or checkout can
# reintroduce it (it did once, at the M1 fix wave -- msys sh masked it).
*.sh text eol=lf
# cmd.exe batch files are the mirror case: label/goto parsing wants CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
# Parity prompt files are consumed as raw bytes (the file IS the prompt;
# MANIFEST token counts are computed over exactly these bytes). Exempt them
# from ALL eol conversion so no checkout can rewrite \n into \r\n and
# silently change tokenization under the parity gate.
tests/data/parity/*.txt -text
# Same rule for the W1 validation prompts: their committed oracle JSON
# captures and ref outputs correspond to these exact bytes.
tests/tooling/validation/*.txt -text
# The HTTP garbage corpus IS its bytes. Several fixtures exist precisely to
# carry a bare LF, a bare CR, or a CRLF split across a read boundary, and eol
# conversion would rewrite the defect out of them on checkout -- turning a
# fixture into a different test while its name still claims the old one. The
# damage would appear only on a fresh clone, never in the tree that wrote them.
tests/data/http_corpus/*.raw -text
